The error you're encountering is due to an input mismatch exception thrown by the Scanner class in Java. This exception occurs when the user enters an invalid input that cannot be parsed as the expected data type.

In your case, the error is occurring at line 7 of the `RetrieveInputExample` class, which is where you're trying to read an integer input from the user using the `Scanner` object. The `nextInt()` method of the `Scanner` class attempts to read an integer value from the input stream, but if the input is not a valid integer, it throws an `InputMismatchException`.

To handle this exception, you can use a try-catch block to catch the exception and provide appropriate error handling. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this exception:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        try {
            int input = scanner.nextInt();
            System.out.println("You entered: " + input);
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid integer.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // Consume the remaining input on the line
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we've added a try-catch block around the code that reads the integer input from the user. If an `InputMismatchException` is thrown, it will be caught by the catch block, and the program will display an error message and consume the remaining input on the line using the `nextLine()` method.

This way, you can handle input mismatch exceptions gracefully and provide a better user experience for your application.

